;;; fold-family-book-sites.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; Companion to fold-family.slatl. That card teaches the fold trap in the
;;; abstract (R5RS / R6RS / Racket / SRFI-1). THIS card preserves the eight
;;; AUTHENTIC broken sites the 2026-07-25 book sweep caught in the wild — the
;;; exact code that lived in a scheme-books/ chapter before it was conformed to
;;; SRFI-1 `fold`/`fold-right`. Each is real muscle-memory output from a
;;; programmer trained on R6RS or Racket. Kept ON PURPOSE as SFT teaching signal
;;; per the keep-broken-lane doctrine: an authentic broken artifact is worth
;;; more than a synthetic one, because it shows the shape the mistake actually
;;; takes in running prose.
;;;
;;; The live chapters were fixed; these originals survive here, in the canonical
;;; broken-examples home, so they are not lost to a _status/ working file.
;;;
;;; Provenance of the sweep: books caught 2026-07-25 —
;;;   book-of-music/ch02 · book-of-challenges/{ch01,ch04,ch12,ch16} ·
;;;   book-of-animation/ch11 · book-of-cs-curriculum/04-trees · book-of-logic/13.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — real fold-family sites caught in the books")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 1. book-of-music/ch02 — the worst case: teaches the excluded name AS the fold
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-music-ch02-foldl-local-def"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("Racket" "R6RS")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-music/ch02-language (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
"(define foldl
  (lambda (fn init lst)
    (if (null? lst)
        init
        (foldl fn (fn init (car lst)) (cdr lst)))))     ;; acc-first: (fn init elem)

(define sum (foldl + 0 (list 1 2 3 4)))                 ;; 10
(define product (foldl * 1 (list 2 3 5)))               ;; 30"
  :broken-prose "\"Here is a left fold: … Fold (also called reduce) …\""
  :runs "yes — but it TEACHES the wrong thing"

  :why
"Three faults in one blow. (a) `foldl` is a Racket name; the local def gives it
the R6RS acc-first order `(fn init elem)`. (b) Motoi already ships SRFI-1 `fold`,
element-first, built in — no local def needed, and shadowing the topic with a
non-standard name is exactly what the standard forbids. (c) The prose calls it
'also called reduce' — but `reduce` is NOT a synonym: SRFI-1 `reduce` ignores
its seed for a non-empty list; `fold` always folds the seed in. A reader learns
the excluded name, the wrong argument order, AND a false synonym at once."

  :fix-code
"(define sum (fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4)))                  ;; 10
(define product (fold * 1 (list 2 3 5)))                ;; 30"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note "Use built-in SRFI-1 `fold`. Drop the local def. Drop the 'also called reduce' framing."
  :lesson "See fold-family.slatl. This is the trap at its most dangerous: a book that DEFINES the excluded name and blesses it in prose.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 2. book-of-challenges/ch01 — fold-left acc-first in solution code (×3)
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-challenges-ch01-fold-left-acc-first"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-challenges/ch01-recursion-warmups (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
";; platform order totals
(fold-left (lambda (acc order)
             (let ((total (cdr (assoc 'total order))))
               (if total (+ acc total) acc)))
           0 orders)"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol: fold-left"

  :why "`fold-left` is R6RS, unbound in Motoi. The lambdas are acc-first `(acc elem)`, R6RS order."
  :fix-code
"(fold (lambda (order acc)
        (let ((total (cdr (assoc 'total order))))
          (if total (+ acc total) acc)))
      0 orders)"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note "SRFI-1 `fold` with element-first lambdas — swap params `(acc order)`->`(order acc)`."
  :lesson "R6RS acc-first -> SRFI-1 elem-first needs BOTH a rename and a parameter-order swap.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 3. book-of-challenges/ch04 — the self-aware shim
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-challenges-ch04-foldr-shim"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("Racket")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-challenges/ch04-list-surgery (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
"(define (group-by-fold key-fn xs)
  (let ((alist (foldr (lambda (item acc) ...) '() xs)))
    ...))

;; Note: many Schemes call right fold 'foldr'. In the runtime's core, assume:
(define (foldr fn init xs)
  (if (null? xs) init (fn (car xs) (foldr fn init (cdr xs)))))"
  :runs "yes — but names the Racket form as if it were ours"

  :why
"The shim is EXACTLY SRFI-1 `fold-right` (element-first) under a non-standard
name. The chapter knew the name was off and shipped a note — but naming it
`foldr` still teaches the Racket name as Motoi's."
  :fix-code
"(define (group-by-fold key-fn xs)
  (fold-right (lambda (item acc) ...) '() xs))"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note "Use built-in `fold-right`. Delete the shim and the note."
  :lesson "A correct implementation under the wrong name is still a broken-example: it teaches the wrong vocabulary.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 4. book-of-challenges/ch12 — the richest case: inconsistent, never ran
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-challenges-ch12-mixed-fold-conventions"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("Racket")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-challenges/ch12-composition-and-macros (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
";; pipe assumed acc-first, maybe-pipe assumed element-first — same file
(define (pipe . fns) (lambda (x) (foldl (lambda (acc f) (f acc)) x fns)))   ;; acc-first
(define (maybe-pipe . fns) (lambda (x) (foldl maybe-apply x fns)))          ;; needs elem-first

;; transducers: two mutually incompatible mental models
(define (mapping f)
  (lambda (reducer) (lambda (acc x) (reducer acc (f x)))))     ;; acc-first reducer
(define (compose-transducers . xfs)
  (foldl (lambda (acc xf) (xf acc)) (lambda (acc x) (cons x acc)) (reverse xfs)))
(define (transduce xf reducer init lst)
  (foldl (xf reducer) init lst))                                ;; xf already a reducer -> 'not a function'"
  :runs "no — foldl unbound; and even bound, produces nested junk, never (4 16)"

  :why
"(a) `foldl`/`foldr` used 15x with no local def -> unbound. (b) acc-first and
element-first conventions mixed within one chapter. (c) `compose-transducers`
returns a reducer but `transduce` expects a transducer and calls `(xf reducer)`.
Classic fold-argument-order confusion compounded across an abstraction — the
single hardest way the fold trap bites."
  :fix-code
"(define (pipe . fns) (lambda (x) (fold (lambda (f acc) (f acc)) x fns)))
(define (compose-transducers . xfs) (fold-right (lambda (xf acc) (xf acc)) (lambda (acc x) (cons x acc)) xfs))
(define (transduce xf reducer init lst) (fold (xf reducer) init lst))"
  :fix-runs "yes — verified -> (4 16)"
  :fix-note "SRFI-1 `fold`/`fold-right` element-first throughout; reducers use `(reducer element acc)`."
  :lesson "When a fold sits under an abstraction (pipe, transducer), a single wrong argument order propagates into type errors far from the fold itself.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 5. book-of-challenges/ch16 — Racket foldl ×14 (elem-first, wrong name only)
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-challenges-ch16-foldl-rename-only"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("Racket")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-challenges/ch16-the-summit (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
"(foldl (lambda (id acc) ...) '() ids)
(foldl + 0 scores)
(foldl reducer '() events)"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol: foldl"

  :why
"14 uses of `foldl`, all element-first `(elem acc)`. `foldl` is a Racket name,
unbound in Motoi — but the ARGUMENT ORDER already matches SRFI-1 (Racket `foldl`
is element-first like `fold`)."
  :fix-code
"(fold (lambda (id acc) ...) '() ids)
(fold + 0 scores)
(fold reducer '() events)"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note "Pure rename `foldl` -> `fold`. NO parameter swap."
  :lesson "The 'same order, different name' corner: least dangerous, because once the name is bound it behaves correctly. Contrast with case 2 (R6RS), where a rename alone is a bug.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 6. book-of-animation/ch11 — R6RS fold-left ×1 (acc-first, commutative body)
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-animation-ch11-fold-left-vector-sum"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-animation/ch11-flocking (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code "(fold-left (lambda (acc obs) ...) '(0 0) obstacles)"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol: fold-left"

  :why "`fold-left` unbound (R6RS); lambda acc-first. Accumulates a 2-vector flee-force over obstacles."
  :fix-code "(fold (lambda (obs acc) ...) '(0 0) obstacles)"
  :fix-runs "yes (uses dialect verb ai/flee — not runnable in base, but conforms)"
  :fix-note "Swap params to element-first. Accumulation is vector-sum (order-independent), so only name+order needed fixing."
  :lesson "Even when the body is commutative and the RESULT would be identical, the name is still unbound — you must rename regardless.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 7. book-of-cs-curriculum/04-trees — fold-left over a bare verb (find-replace BUG)
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-cs-04-trees-fold-left-bst-insert"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-cs-curriculum/04-trees (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code "(fold-left bst-insert '() '(5 3 8 1 4 7 9))"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol: fold-left"

  :why
"`fold-left` unbound. AND `bst-insert` is `(tree value)` — tree-first, i.e.
acc-first, matching `fold-left`'s `(kons acc elem)`. A blind rename to `fold`
would pass `(value tree)` to a `(tree value)` verb -> arguments reversed -> wrong
tree. This is precisely the case a find-replace SILENTLY breaks."
  :fix-code "(fold (lambda (v tree) (bst-insert tree v)) '() '(5 3 8 1 4 7 9))"
  :fix-runs "yes — verified, in-order traversal (1 3 4 5 7 8 9)"
  :fix-note "Wrap to restore the verb's argument order under an element-first fold."
  :lesson "When the folded function is a bare non-commutative verb, you cannot rename OR blind-swap — you must wrap to preserve its argument order. The most dangerous rename in the family.")

;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;; 8. book-of-logic/13 — fold-left ×1 + a metadata leak
;; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(broken-example :name "book-logic-13-fold-left-plus-metadata-leak"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "the-fold-family"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :std-rejected ("R6RS")
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme-books/book-of-logic/13-non-monotonic-and-default-logic (as of 2026-07-24)"

  :broken-code
";; in add-fireable-defaults
(fold-left (lambda (acc d) ...) facts defaults)
;; AND the chapter's metadata advertised the excluded name:
:verbs-used (... \"fold-left\" ...)"
  :runs "no — unbound symbol: fold-left"

  :why
"`fold-left` unbound; lambda acc-first; AND `:verbs-used` metadata listed the
excluded name, so an index built from metadata would advertise a verb that does
not exist."
  :fix-code
"(fold (lambda (d acc) ...) facts defaults)
:verbs-used (... \"fold\" ...)"
  :fix-runs "yes — verified -> (has-feathers flies bird)"
  :fix-note
"Swap params to element-first. SRFI-1 `fold` is a LEFT fold (same left-to-right
traversal as `fold-left`), so the fixpoint semantics are preserved; only the
kons argument order flips. Also correct `:verbs-used` to `\"fold\"`."
  :lesson "Conformance is not only about code that runs — machine-readable metadata must not advertise excluded names either, or downstream indexes inherit the mistake.")
